  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1121 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nPretty quiet across the area as of 11 PM, with earlier rain \nhaving largely dissipated. However, additional showers and \nstorms are still on track to move into portions of the area \nThursday morning as another shortwave trough slides through and \nlow level moisture transport points toward the area. Little to \nno instability will be in place, so thunderstorms chances will \nbe quite low, but any showers that do develop could be somewhat\nefficient rain producers with precipitable water values \nclimbing over 1.75\" near the NE/KS border and warm cloud depths \nover 4 km. That said, latest guidance has trended toward keeping\nthe heavier precip farther southwest, with only a 10% chance of\nseeing at least 1\" clipping Jefferson County. In fact, now only\nexpecting a few tenths of an inch at most southwest of a line \nfrom Albion to Beatrice, with maybe only a few hundredths to the\nnortheast. Despite the downward trend in precip chances, \ntemperatures look to remain on the cooler side, with highs in \nthe mid 70s to lower 80s.\n\nWhile any showers should dissipate through Thursday afternoon, \nwe'll once again see shower and storm redevelopment Thursday \nevening into early Friday as a stronger shortwave trough moves \nthrough with low level moisture transport pointing into \nsoutheast NE. Like tonight, trends with the heaviest \nprecipitation have been a little farther south. Elevated \nprecipitable water values (near 2\") and warm cloud depths over \n4.5 km suggest we'll have some efficient rain producers. In \naddition, it looks like we'll have maybe a little more \ninstability, at least along the NE/KS border, so could see \ntotals over 1\" in that area, with some localized 2+\" amounts. \nWe'll have to see how things trend through the day on Thursday. \nOtherwise, precip will taper off through the day with lingering \nclouds giving us one more day of highs in the upper 70s to mid \n80s for most.\n\nHeat will then build back in for the weekend and into next week\nas ridging to our west will amplify and edge eastward. NAEFS \nand EPS guidance shows 850 mb temperatures climbing into the mid\nand then upper 20s Saturday and Sunday, good for the 90th+ \npercentile of climatology. Still some questions on how deep \nwe'll mix with stark differences between EPS and GEFS guidance. \nGEFS members are almost certainly over-mixed with temperatures \nwell into the 100s area-wide. EPS seems more reasonable with \nhighs in the mid 90s to around 100, but with our tendency to mix\na little deeper on anomalously warm days would lean toward \nslightly higher temperatures than what EPS is showing. In \naddition, between moisture advection and corn \nevapotranspiration, dewpoints look to climb back into the 70s \nand lead to heat indices in at least parts of the area Saturday \nthrough Monday. Sunday currently looks like the hottest day, \nwith many locations seeing heat indices of 105 to 110.\n\nAll that said, one fly in the ointment on the temperature \nforecast will be potential for various bits of shortwave energy \nto round the ridge and slide through the forecast area giving us\nchances for showers and storms, or at the very least, clouds. \nStill quite a bit of spread in timing, track, and strength of \nthese disturbances, but currently looking at occasional 10-20% \nchances, especially in northeast NE into west-central IA Sunday \ninto next week.",
